WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.952
This briefing comes from Information
Protection and Microsoft Purview,

3
00:00:04.952 --> 00:00:07.562
a book of the Federal
Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.562 --> 00:00:10.212
One thing first: the book is a draft.

5
00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:13.264
Every page is awaiting
the editor's review,

6
00:00:13.264 --> 00:00:16.926
and it isn't part of Release
2.0, the baseline edition.

7
00:00:16.926 --> 00:00:20.502
It's written for C I Os
and CISOs, data owners,

8
00:00:20.502 --> 00:00:25.613
records and privacy officials,
Purview engineers, and ISSOs.

9
00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:27.026
I'll take four questions.

10
00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:29.425
Why does protection have
to travel with the data?

11
00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:32.113
What does the program build
with Microsoft Purview?

12
00:00:32.113 --> 00:00:33.024
Who runs it?

13
00:00:33.024 --> 00:00:35.400
And how do you roll it
out and prove it works?

14
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.975
The short version: identity, device and
network controls decide who reaches a file.

15
00:00:40.975 --> 00:00:44.891
Only a classification decided in
advance can say what the file is,

16
00:00:44.891 --> 00:00:49.740
and that decision has to be written down
before a single label is published.

17
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:58.890
A Zero Trust program usually builds
identity, devices and the network first.

18
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:03.202
Each of those controls passes a
legitimate user on a compliant device

19
00:01:03.202 --> 00:01:07.290
sending a file to an approved
destination, whatever the file holds.

20
00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.665
Nothing upstream said
what the payload was.

21
00:01:09.665 --> 00:01:14.790
CISA's Zero Trust Maturity Model makes the
same point with a Data pillar of its own.

22
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.690
Three things make perimeter
thinking fail for data.

23
00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:22.951
It moves more than people do: an export
becomes an attachment, a OneDrive file,

24
00:01:22.951 --> 00:01:24.956
a chat paste and an AI summary,

25
00:01:24.956 --> 00:01:29.215
and each copy inherits none of
the original's access controls.

26
00:01:29.215 --> 00:01:32.665
Access is granted to
containers, not to content.

27
00:01:32.665 --> 00:01:35.540
And the agency can't protect
what it hasn't named.

28
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.265
Microsoft's sensitivity label
is what lets protection travel.

29
00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:43.300
It's stored in clear text in the
item's metadata, it stays with the

30
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.490
content wherever it's saved, and
so does any encryption it applies.

31
00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.290
But a label is only as good
as the class it stands for.

32
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.702
So the first deliverable is a set of
written decisions, not a label policy.

33
00:01:59.702 --> 00:02:02.281
A data class is the
agency's name for a kind

34
00:02:02.281 --> 00:02:05.315
of information that needs
one handling outcome.

35
00:02:05.315 --> 00:02:09.842
The class register lists them, one
row each, and every later label,

36
00:02:09.842 --> 00:02:14.028
D L P rule and auto-labeling
policy cites the class it serves.

37
00:02:14.028 --> 00:02:18.346
Each class has a data owner, an
official rather than an administrator,

38
00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:21.528
who decides its handling
and approves exceptions.

39
00:02:21.528 --> 00:02:25.003
A federal agency inherits
several of these decisions.

40
00:02:25.003 --> 00:02:29.261
The C U I rule, thirty-two C F R
part two thousand two, and the C U I

41
00:02:29.261 --> 00:02:34.564
Registry decide which categories the
agency holds and how they're marked.

42
00:02:34.564 --> 00:02:39.440
Category markings are optional for C
U I Basic and mandatory for Specified.

43
00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.628
The Privacy Act, records schedules and
fips one ninety-nine decide the rest,

44
00:02:44.628 --> 00:02:48.477
and C U I Basic sets a
confidentiality floor of moderate.

45
00:02:48.477 --> 00:02:50.602
And each outcome has to be testable.

46
00:02:50.602 --> 00:02:52.414
Sensitive isn't an outcome.

47
00:02:52.414 --> 00:02:58.270
Encrypted so only a named group can open
it, and never sent outside the tenant, is.

48
00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.532
The book's reference
architecture has three

49
00:03:04.532 --> 00:03:06.908
trust boundaries and four control planes.

50
00:03:06.908 --> 00:03:11.049
The boundaries are the tenant,
the managed endpoint, and outside,

51
00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:15.033
where external recipients can't
see the agency's labels at all.

52
00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:20.151
The planes are identity, which owns the
groups that labels grant rights to; policy,

53
00:03:20.151 --> 00:03:24.887
which is Purview; protection, which is
Rights Management and the tenant key;

54
00:03:24.887 --> 00:03:27.332
and monitoring, which is Purview Audit.

55
00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:29.008
Each plane has its own owner.

56
00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:33.170
The label design follows the class
register, one label per class.

57
00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:38.944
Microsoft reports that effectiveness
drops noticeably beyond five main labels,

58
00:03:38.944 --> 00:03:44.039
or five sublabels under one, so the
taxonomy stays small: Public, Internal,

59
00:03:44.039 --> 00:03:48.880
C U I Basic, a category sublabel only
where agency policy requires one,

60
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.720
and one Specified sublabel per
authority the agency actually holds.

61
00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.508
Container labels sit in
a group of their own.

62
00:03:56.508 --> 00:04:01.120
Encryption grants rights to mail-enabled
groups, never to individuals.

63
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.570
And a label that doesn't encrypt
is a classification signal.

64
00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:08.270
Once the content leaves the
tenant, it protects nothing.

65
00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:14.999
A label does nothing until
a policy publishes it,

66
00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:17.233
and the policy carries the defaults.

67
00:04:17.233 --> 00:04:21.482
The program defaults documents,
email and meetings to Internal,

68
00:04:21.482 --> 00:04:24.208
and new groups and sites to Internal site.

69
00:04:24.208 --> 00:04:28.316
None of those encrypts, because
Microsoft cautions that an encrypting

70
00:04:28.316 --> 00:04:32.146
default may leave external recipients
unable to open the content.

71
00:04:32.146 --> 00:04:36.380
Mandatory labeling then makes users
label before they save or send,

72
00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.632
and with a sensible default
they're rarely prompted.

73
00:04:39.632 --> 00:04:42.115
A user can change a
label but not remove it,

74
00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:46.058
and the program asks for a
justification when someone lowers one.

75
00:04:46.058 --> 00:04:49.162
Containers get three labels of their own:

76
00:04:49.162 --> 00:04:52.632
Internal site, CUI site
and CUI Specified site.

77
00:04:52.632 --> 00:04:56.060
Each sets privacy, guest
access, external sharing,

78
00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.821
unmanaged-device access and
the default sharing link.

79
00:04:59.821 --> 00:05:02.449
But a container label governs the site,

80
00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:05.570
not the files: the items
inside don't inherit it.

81
00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:10.508
In GCC, file labels and container
labels are listed as available.

82
00:05:10.508 --> 00:05:14.653
The default label for a SharePoint
library is still in development there,

83
00:05:14.653 --> 00:05:19.420
and authentication contexts on labeled
sites are on the engineering backlog.

84
00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:26.420
Encryption is where a
mistake is hardest to undo.

85
00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:30.730
Rights Management is usually
already on: Microsoft activated it

86
00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:35.120
automatically for subscriptions
from late February 2018 onward.

87
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.174
So the decisions are who may encrypt
during rollout, which tenant key,

88
00:05:39.174 --> 00:05:43.157
and who can recover content when
its only authorized holder is gone.

89
00:05:43.157 --> 00:05:47.720
The program keeps the super user
feature off, with no standing members.

90
00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:53.160
For a named recovery, an operator activates
an approved role through PIM, turns

91
00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:58.683
the feature on, adds one named super user,
recovers the item, and then removes both.

92
00:05:58.683 --> 00:06:00.520
Not through a PIM-managed group,

93
00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.407
because the service caches group
membership for up to three hours.

94
00:06:04.407 --> 00:06:06.233
Mail uses the same service.

95
00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:10.631
Labels carry classification, and mail
flow rules carry approved scenarios,

96
00:06:10.631 --> 00:06:13.144
so neither depends on
a sender remembering.

97
00:06:13.144 --> 00:06:19.300
In SharePoint and OneDrive, turn label
integration on before encrypting labels go

98
00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.920
wide; until then, encrypted files are
invisible to search, eDiscovery and D L P.

99
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.414
In GCC, Rights Management,
message encryption and

100
00:06:28.414 --> 00:06:32.780
co-authoring on encrypted
files are listed as available.

101
00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:40.918
Microsoft's D L P templates are a
convenient start and a poor design.

102
00:06:40.918 --> 00:06:45.768
The program writes each policy from an
outcome statement the policy owner owns.

103
00:06:45.768 --> 00:06:50.837
For example: content labeled C U I isn't
shared or sent outside the tenant except

104
00:06:50.837 --> 00:06:54.504
through the approved partner
path; an attempt is blocked,

105
00:06:54.504 --> 00:06:58.092
the user is told why, and the
security monitor is alerted.

106
00:06:58.092 --> 00:07:01.918
Rules match labels first, a
decision someone already made,

107
00:07:01.918 --> 00:07:05.106
and treat raw content
patterns as the safety net.

108
00:07:05.106 --> 00:07:10.006
Overrides are logged and read every
week, and exceptions name groups,

109
00:07:10.006 --> 00:07:12.930
sit in a register, and
expire within a year.

110
00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:19.742
Endpoint D L P covers Windows 10 and
11, and Windows Server 2019 and later.

111
00:07:19.742 --> 00:07:24.754
For content nobody labeled, auto-labeling
climbs a ladder: built-in types,

112
00:07:24.754 --> 00:07:28.846
custom types, exact data match,
then trainable classifiers,

113
00:07:28.846 --> 00:07:31.518
only as far as false positives require.

114
00:07:31.518 --> 00:07:35.582
Client-side labeling recommends
a label while the author works;

115
00:07:35.582 --> 00:07:38.956
service-side labeling applies
one at rest and in mail.

116
00:07:38.956 --> 00:07:41.345
In GCC, D L P on every surface,

117
00:07:41.345 --> 00:07:47.030
auto-labeling and exact data
match are listed as available.

118
00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:52.943
Every other signal depends on audit.

119
00:07:52.943 --> 00:07:56.228
Audit Standard is on by
default for enterprise

120
00:07:56.228 --> 00:07:59.429
organizations and keeps
records for 180 days.

121
00:07:59.429 --> 00:08:03.043
Audit Premium keeps the core
services' records for a year,

122
00:08:03.043 --> 00:08:05.042
and up to ten years with an add-on.

123
00:08:05.042 --> 00:08:08.761
But retention follows the
license of the user who acted,

124
00:08:08.761 --> 00:08:13.692
so the agency's log platform, not the
tenant, holds the full evidence period.

125
00:08:13.692 --> 00:08:15.555
Search isn't real time, either:

126
00:08:15.555 --> 00:08:19.280
typically sixty to ninety
minutes for the main workloads.

127
00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.617
The program proves the pipeline every
month with a synthetic label event,

128
00:08:23.617 --> 00:08:26.105
found in the tenant and
in the log platform.

129
00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.488
Insider Risk Management and Communication

130
00:08:28.488 --> 00:08:31.193
Compliance are different:
they watch named people.

131
00:08:31.193 --> 00:08:35.179
So before either is configured,
the agency signs a charter.

132
00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:37.132
The CISO, the privacy official,

133
00:08:37.132 --> 00:08:41.863
general counsel and labor relations
settle the purpose, the notice and what

134
00:08:41.863 --> 00:08:46.518
investigators may see, and the records
officer sets how long cases are kept.

135
00:08:46.518 --> 00:08:49.855
Pseudonyms stay on,
administrators don't investigate,

136
00:08:49.855 --> 00:08:53.043
and someone outside the
team reviews the reviewers.

137
00:08:53.043 --> 00:08:59.550
In GCC, both audit tiers and the core insider
risk features are listed as available.

138
00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.012
AI changes the problem, not the controls.

139
00:09:06.012 --> 00:09:09.750
Microsoft describes Copilot's
protection in a fixed order.

140
00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:14.463
First, permissions: it only uses
content the user can already reach.

141
00:09:14.463 --> 00:09:19.744
Second, encryption: the user needs both
the VIEW and the EXTRACT usage rights for

142
00:09:19.744 --> 00:09:22.384
Copilot to summarize an encrypted item,

143
00:09:22.384 --> 00:09:26.225
so the usage rights in each
sublabel are now an AI decision.

144
00:09:26.225 --> 00:09:30.072
Third, labels: Copilot shows
the label of what it drew on,

145
00:09:30.072 --> 00:09:33.663
and new content inherits
the highest-priority one.

146
00:09:33.663 --> 00:09:36.125
That makes oversharing a disclosure path.

147
00:09:36.125 --> 00:09:39.772
A site shared with the whole
agency was always readable;

148
00:09:39.772 --> 00:09:41.912
an assistant finds it in seconds.

149
00:09:41.912 --> 00:09:46.486
So the policy owner decides four things
first: which AI apps are approved,

150
00:09:46.486 --> 00:09:50.069
which labels Copilot may
process, what oversharing gets

151
00:09:50.069 --> 00:09:53.575
fixed before rollout, and who
may read captured prompts.

152
00:09:53.575 --> 00:09:57.975
D L P for Copilot can keep chosen
labels out of its responses,

153
00:09:57.975 --> 00:10:02.375
though excluding files and
email needs E5-level licensing.

154
00:10:02.375 --> 00:10:10.220
In GCC, Microsoft 365 Copilot and Purview's
controls for it are listed as available.

155
00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:19.423
Purview differs a great deal between clouds,
and changes often, so the book states

156
00:10:19.423 --> 00:10:24.958
availability feature by feature, as Microsoft
Learn listed it on November 11, 2026.

157
00:10:24.958 --> 00:10:27.557
The baseline here is GCC.

158
00:10:27.557 --> 00:10:32.850
There, the core is listed as available:
manual, mandatory and container labels,

159
00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:37.978
Rights Management and message encryption,
D L P on every surface including

160
00:10:37.978 --> 00:10:42.857
Windows endpoints, auto-labeling,
exact data match, and both audit tiers.

161
00:10:42.857 --> 00:10:44.183
A few gaps matter.

162
00:10:44.183 --> 00:10:48.052
The default label for a SharePoint
library is still in development,

163
00:10:48.052 --> 00:10:52.545
and authentication contexts on labeled
sites are on the engineering backlog.

164
00:10:52.545 --> 00:10:55.833
GCC High and DoD are side notes.

165
00:10:55.833 --> 00:11:01.315
Their clients need DNS records to reach
the government Rights Management service,

166
00:11:01.315 --> 00:11:04.572
DoD doesn't list Advanced
Message Encryption yet,

167
00:11:04.572 --> 00:11:09.021
and two Learn pages disagree on
Insider Risk Management in GCC High.

168
00:11:09.021 --> 00:11:12.250
The rule for all of it: a
license or cloud gap goes

169
00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:15.645
in a decision record, with
a compensating control.

170
00:11:15.645 --> 00:11:18.640
It's a dependency, never a failed control.

171
00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:25.509
Information protection
fails quietly when the

172
00:11:25.509 --> 00:11:28.940
person who decides is also
the person who configures.

173
00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:32.977
The book uses six roles,
by name, in every chapter.

174
00:11:32.977 --> 00:11:37.897
The policy owner, typically the C U I
program manager, with the privacy official

175
00:11:37.897 --> 00:11:42.514
and the records officer, decides what
the data is and how it must be handled.

176
00:11:42.514 --> 00:11:45.401
The platform owner, the
Purview engineering lead,

177
00:11:45.401 --> 00:11:48.640
is accountable for the
configuration and its baseline.

178
00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:51.852
The operator makes each change
through change control.

179
00:11:51.852 --> 00:11:55.027
The security monitor
triages alerts every day.

180
00:11:55.027 --> 00:11:56.883
The help desk answers users,

181
00:11:56.883 --> 00:12:00.916
and never removes a label or
decrypts a file to close a ticket.

182
00:12:00.916 --> 00:12:05.334
And the assessor, the ISSO and the
independent assessor, is accountable

183
00:12:05.334 --> 00:12:10.128
for the evidence package, while closure
stays with the authorizing official.

184
00:12:10.128 --> 00:12:12.103
Three separations keep it honest.

185
00:12:12.103 --> 00:12:14.853
Owners decide and
administrators configure.

186
00:12:14.853 --> 00:12:17.892
Super user recovery answers
to the policy owner,

187
00:12:17.892 --> 00:12:20.703
because it reads content
that owner governs.

188
00:12:20.703 --> 00:12:25.280
And in insider risk, no one both
administers and investigates.

189
00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:33.817
Every control in the book follows the
same path, and every step has a way back.

190
00:12:33.817 --> 00:12:35.356
Start with an inventory.

191
00:12:35.356 --> 00:12:40.067
Purview's content explorer exports
where sensitive information types and

192
00:12:40.067 --> 00:12:44.705
labels already sit, one type at a time,
so the rollout starts where the risk is.

193
00:12:44.705 --> 00:12:46.586
Treat it as a dated snapshot:

194
00:12:46.586 --> 00:12:51.330
counts can take up to seven days to
update, and fourteen for SharePoint.

195
00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:54.815
Then a signed decision record,
and the build, as code,

196
00:12:54.815 --> 00:12:59.793
in a test tenant first, because some
label changes can't be cleanly undone.

197
00:12:59.793 --> 00:13:01.004
Then a pilot ring.

198
00:13:01.004 --> 00:13:04.080
For D L P, simulation is the pilot.

199
00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.555
It runs for up to fifteen days and keeps
its data for thirty, so export the matches.

200
00:13:09.555 --> 00:13:14.384
It evaluates existing SharePoint and
OneDrive content, but only new mail,

201
00:13:14.384 --> 00:13:18.817
Teams and device activity, so let
those run through a business cycle.

202
00:13:18.817 --> 00:13:22.792
Then enforce ring by ring, and
validate with a labeled sample.

203
00:13:22.792 --> 00:13:26.892
And rollback never means deleting
a label, because content keeps it.

204
00:13:26.892 --> 00:13:28.920
You unpublish it instead.

205
00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.808
Everything ends in evidence
an assessor can inspect.

206
00:13:35.808 --> 00:13:41.149
The baseline is one dated export of the
labels, label policies, auto-labeling,

207
00:13:41.149 --> 00:13:46.320
D L P, audit status and the Rights
Management configuration, taken monthly.

208
00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:49.666
Drift is any difference no
change ticket explains,

209
00:13:49.666 --> 00:13:54.445
and drift that weakens a control on C
U I goes to the authorizing official.

210
00:13:54.445 --> 00:13:58.951
The book carries three rows of the
Zero Trust Assessment closure map,

211
00:13:58.951 --> 00:14:01.007
each with its minimum evidence.

212
00:14:01.007 --> 00:14:06.270
No labels, defaults or containers needs
a policy export and a labeled sample.

213
00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:09.119
Rights Management and
mail encryption need an

214
00:14:09.119 --> 00:14:11.895
encrypted test and the
transport-rule export.

215
00:14:11.895 --> 00:14:14.845
Audit gaps need searchable
synthetic events.

216
00:14:14.845 --> 00:14:18.093
And the metrics measure
protection, not settings:

217
00:14:18.093 --> 00:14:22.847
the labeled share of sensitive content,
container coverage of C U I sites,

218
00:14:22.847 --> 00:14:26.095
true-positive and override
rates, and test passes.

219
00:14:26.095 --> 00:14:30.608
A policy that's on and catches nothing
scores no better than one that's off.

220
00:14:30.608 --> 00:14:33.510
The package supports
the controls it maps to;

221
00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:36.930
the assessor or authorizing
official decides.

222
00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:42.055
So, four decisions.

223
00:14:42.055 --> 00:14:47.122
Name the classes and their owners: a small
register built from the C U I Registry,

224
00:14:47.122 --> 00:14:51.955
the Privacy Act and records schedules,
with an outcome an assessor can test.

225
00:14:51.955 --> 00:14:56.475
Publish a small taxonomy with safe
defaults: non-encrypting defaults,

226
00:14:56.475 --> 00:14:59.593
mandatory labeling, and
three container labels.

227
00:14:59.593 --> 00:15:02.997
Keep a tested way back in:
the super user feature off,

228
00:15:02.997 --> 00:15:07.780
recovery through an approved role,
and a decryption test every quarter.

229
00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:12.578
And decide AI and evidence before rollout:
which labels Copilot may process,

230
00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:17.605
what oversharing gets fixed first, and a
monthly baseline the assessor can read.

231
00:15:17.605 --> 00:15:19.755
The next step needs no new tooling.

232
00:15:19.755 --> 00:15:22.732
Draft the class register
and the decision records,

233
00:15:22.732 --> 00:15:26.277
run a dated inventory with what
the tenant already licenses,

234
00:15:26.277 --> 00:15:30.105
and confirm each feature for your
own cloud before planning on it.

235
00:15:30.105 --> 00:15:32.268
Protection has to travel with the data.

236
00:15:32.268 --> 00:15:37.781
Information Protection and Microsoft Purview
has the detail, every claim footnoted.

237
00:15:37.781 --> 00:15:41.293
It's a draft, so check it again
after the editor's review.

238
00:15:41.293 --> 00:15:42.120
Thank you.
